    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d97-a1">

                <pb facs="0246" n="146" sameAs="frus:frus1884/pg146"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure in No. 477.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Harrison to Mr. Frelinghuysen.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >United States Senate</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1884-03-21"><hi rendition="#italic">March</hi>, 21,
                        1884</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to inclose the sworn
                    statement of John G. Foichat, a citizen of the United States and a resident of
                    Parke County, Indiana; also the letter of Ared F. White, Esq., an
                    attorney-at-law at Rockville, Parke Co., Ind. It seems clear from the statement
                    made by Mr. Foichat that his rights as an American citizen have been wantonly
                    and grossly invaded by the French authorities. I respectfully ask that such
                    steps may be taken by your Department as are necessary to a fall investigation
                    of this case, and if the facts are found to be as stated by Mr. Foichat, that a
                    proper demand for reparation may be made.</p>
                <p>Very respectfully, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>BENJ. HARRISON.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
